{"id": "sevt_01WrV5FU3Rd1ytr57wq81qYG", "input": {"evidence": "tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:10 builds an INSERT by concatenating `name` and `email`. Those values come only from the loop at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:9 over the module constant USERS, defined at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:3 as hardcoded literals (\"alice\"/\"bob\" plus example.com emails). The only parameter of seed(), `path` (tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:6), goes to sqlite3.connect at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:7 and not into SQL text. A repo-wide search found no caller of seed() and no import of seed_db outside the definition itself at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:6. The only application code is a Flask /health route that returns a static dict (app/main.py:6-8) and does not import the fixture. config.py:1 only imports os and does not reference the fixture. No attacker-controlled input reaches the query, and the file is test-only code, not deployed request-handling code.", "finding_id": "F-105", "recommended_fix": "No security fix is needed. As a hygiene change, use parameterized queries so the pattern is not copied into production code and the scanner stops flagging it: conn.executemany(\"INSERT INTO users VALUES (?, ?)\", USERS). You could also exclude tests/ from SAST production-severity rules.", "severity": "none", "title": "SQL string concatenation in test fixture seed_db.py uses only hardcoded constants (not exploitable)", "verdict": "not_exploitable"}, "name": "create_ticket_draft", "processed_at": "2026-10-26T20:54:14.374341Z", "type": "agent.custom_tool_use"}
